== Overview <sec:iot_overview>
From the firsts computational machines introduced in the mid of the 20th century to today, the evolution of computer science has seen a continuous innovation and rapid technological advancements. With the advent of internet by the end of the 20th century, the way we interact with technology has profoundly changed the world around us under various aspects, like social interactions, economical forces, political dynamics or the way we access and share information in the everyday life.\
In the past decades, one of the most significant developments in this field is the rise of the Internet of Things (IoT) paradigm. \
This concept refers to a world where everyday objects, such as healthcare sensors, industrial actuators, home appliances and so on, are interconnected through the internet, enabling them to collect and exchange data for a wide range of applications that improve our daily life, through automation, remote sensing and control.
Since its introduction, IoT devices have seen a rapid growth and large adoption in very different areas, from smart homes to industrial automation, healthcare, agriculture and many others.\
The history of IoT started way back in 1982 when a vending machine was connected to the internet and set to display the number of bottles with its temperature. A decade later, in 1991, Mark Weiser introduced the concept of ubiquitous computing @ubiquitosus_computing, which was just a theoretical expression of what IoT is today. The first accepted technology used in this field was the Radio Frequency Identification (RFID) in 1998, a wireless technology using radio waves to automatically identify and track tags attached to objects. Exactly from here, Kevin Ashton, at the time executive director of the Auto-ID center at the MIT, in 1999 @ashton2009internet during a speech about the new idea of using RFID in the P&G's supply chain, coined the term "Internet of Things".
The Auto-ID center was a collaboration between industries and private sectors, holding 60 researchers and 15 professional leading research networks in IoT. The first conference on IoT was organized and held in 2008, and from there many countries embraced it and drafted action plans on the IoT. Later, in 2014, Auto-ID lab released a state-of-the-art report on IoT @auto-id-lab.\
At the beginning, there has still been skepticism about embracing IoT until when tech giants that made IoT big business opportunities. As the market was adopting gradually towards IoT solutions, the number of connected devices started to grow exponentially. Various report projected that 25 billion devices were connected before 2020 @fehske2011global, increasing to 38 billion by the end of 2025 and 50 billion by the end of 2030 @chui2021internet. According to a market research estimate, the global IoT industry was worth 245 billion dollars in 2020 and is predicted to grow to 8,131 billions by 2030 @al2020internet.\ \
[...]
 
However, due to the presence of an important number of heterogeneous and resources constrained devices (in terms of memory, CPU and bandwidth) communicating over error-prone and lossy radio channels and often deployed in hostile environments (e.g. war zone), IoT networks are experiencing various network performance problems (e.g. excessive energy consumption resulting from network device failure). This has fueled the development of different protocols and frameworks for management of IoT networks.
